  "title": "Document Chunking Strategies and Retrieval Quality 2025 (J)",
  "content": {
   "raw_text": "The problem. Embedding an entire long document into one vector averages away the specific passage that answers a query. Splitting into passages restores that specificity but risks cutting the answer in half at a boundary.\n\nOverlap. Overlapping windows of 180 words with 50 words of overlap ensure any sentence appears whole in at least one chunk. The cost is a larger index, roughly proportional to the overlap fraction.\n\nAggregation. A document is scored by its strongest chunk rather than the mean of its chunks. Averaging penalises long documents that answer the query in one place and say nothing relevant elsewhere.",
   "language": "en",
   "word_count": 100,
   "character_count": 620
  },
